{
  "image_name": "0b5ab0730ebe6f8e78a3861479ba797f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different parts of a wind turbine are built from different materials and what recycling routes are available for each. On the left, a wind turbine is broken into major components such as the nacelle, generator or turbine, gearbox, rotor blades and connections, tower, and foundation. It also contrasts two drivetrain designs inside the nacelle: direct drive and geared.\n\nColored linking lines connect each turbine part to the materials used, showing that the system mixes metals, composites, polymers, and concrete. Aluminium, cast iron, chrome steel, steel, concrete, glass-fibre composite, carbon-fibre composite, polymers, and rare earth elements in NdFeB magnets are all represented. The nacelle, gearbox, and generator appear especially metal-intensive, while blades are dominated by fibre composites and polymers, and the foundation is mainly concrete and steel.\n\nThe right side maps these materials to recycling approaches. Metals are mostly routed to remelting or electric arc furnace recycling. Concrete is shown as being crushed for aggregate or processed for cement-related reuse. Composite blade materials have more varied and limited options, including reuse, grinding, cement kiln routes, and thermal or chemical recycling. Rare earth magnets are associated with reuse and short-, medium-, or long-loop recovery processes.",
